<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Bug</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bug River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Bug is a river of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, about 770 kilometres long, rising in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and flowing north along the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> with Ukraine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> before joining the Narew. Its basin held the medieval lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> and the Cherven towns, contested between Poland and Rus', and towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a> stand on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-bug.jpg" alt="Western Bug" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Damian Panasiuk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wyszkow_Bug.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Bug rises in the Lviv Oblast in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and flows north into the Volyn Oblast. It then follows the border between Ukraine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and then that between Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, where it runs along part of the boundary between the Masovian and Podlaskie Voivodeships, and joins the Narew at Serock. Its total length is 774 kilometres. It forms part of the boundary between Belarus and Poland for 178 kilometres and part of that between Ukraine and Poland for 185 kilometres. Its drainage basin covers 38,712 square kilometres, half in Poland, just over a quarter in Belarus and slightly under a quarter in Ukraine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Slavic river name Bug is explained by Zbigniew Gołąb as deriving from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning to bend or turn, so that it originally referred to a river bend. The river has often served as a frontier. Its line has traditionally been treated as the ethnographic border between East and West and between Orthodox Ukrainians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a> and Catholic Poles, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podlachia">Podlachia</a> a borderland where these peoples mixed.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> in 1795 the Bug was part of the frontier between the territories held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. It was later part of the eastern border of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and of Congress Poland, and then of the Vistula Land. A clause of the German-Soviet Frontier Treaty of 28 September 1939, signed after the invasion of Poland, made it part of the dividing line between the German Wehrmacht and the Soviet Red Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Bug is linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> by the Dnieper-Bug Canal, which joins it through the Mukhavets. A few kilometres from Vysokaye in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>'s Brest Region, on the Bug, lies the westernmost point of Belarus. Zegrze Lake, an artificial lake with a hydroelectric complex, was built a little downstream of the confluence with the Narew in 1963. The river floods each spring as the snow melts, and ice jams in winter can raise its level by up to 2 metres.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bug (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Belz, on a tributary of the <b>Bug</b>, was one of the Cherven towns contested between Poland and Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-belz">Principality of Belz</a></li><li><blockquote>Volhynia, between the <b>Bug</b> and the upper Pripyat, was an old land of Rus' with its centre at Volodymyr.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-volhynia">Principality of Volhynia</a></li></ul></section>
